#doc (Manual) "The Orlik–Solomon algebra and its realization" =>
%%%
tag := "presentation"
file := "presentation"
%%%

The two families of relations are different in kind, and the nonempty-intersection condition on the second family is essential.
For two distinct points in $`\mathbb{A}^1` the relation is $`e_1e_2=0`, and $`e_2-e_1` is nonzero over any nonzero coefficient ring.

*Why the relations hold.*
The classes $`[f_i]` are pulled back from a target of small dimension, and the relation holds on that target because its cohomology vanishes above its dimension.
If $`H_1,\ldots,H_s` have empty intersection, then the image of $`(f_1,\ldots,f_s)` is an affine subspace of $`\mathbb{A}^s` that avoids the origin, so it has dimension at most $`s-1`.
The map sends $`U_{\mathcal{A}}` to the complement of the coordinate hyperplanes in that subspace, where a product of $`s` classes has degree $`s` in dimension at most $`s-1` and therefore vanishes.
If the intersection is nonempty of codimension $`r<s`, the ratios $`f_i/f_1` map to a slice of dimension $`r-1`, so the product of the $`s-1` classes $`[f_i/f_1]=[f_i]-[f_1]` vanishes, and expanding that product gives exactly the signed boundary.

These targets are arrangement complements with no relation to the cardinality of $`\mathcal{A}`, which is why {bp "thm:generation"}[the dimension bound] has to be known for all arrangements first.
The factorizations are maps of schemes, so they remain valid when $`U_{\mathcal{A}}` has no $`k`-points.

:::node "def:os" (decl := OrlikSolomon.Arrangement.IsDependent) (decl := OrlikSolomon.osRelations) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.generator_sq) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.isGradedCommutative_bigrading)
The algebra $`\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A})` is the quotient of the exterior algebra over $`B` on generators $`e_H` by the two-sided ideal generated by $`e_I` when $`\bigcap I=\varnothing`, and by $`\partial e_I` when $`\bigcap I\ne\varnothing` has codimension less than the length of $`I`, for all ordered lists $`I` of distinct hyperplanes.
It is graded commutative.
:::

:::node "lem:os-basic" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.bigrading) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.bigradingGradedAlgebra) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.generator_mem_bigrading) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.algebraMap_mem_bigrading) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.deletionMap) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.deletionMap_isHomogeneous) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.baseChangeEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.bigradingComponentEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.bigrading_eq_bot_of_fst_neg)
The algebra has an internal bigrading with $`e_H` in bidegree $`(1,1)` and $`B_w` in bidegree $`(0,w)`.
Deletion gives a homogeneous algebra map $`a:\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A}')\to\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A})`, whose injectivity is not yet known at this stage.
For every homomorphism of commutative rings $`R\to B`, there is a canonical equivalence $`B\otimes_R\operatorname{OS}_R(\mathcal{A})\simeq\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A})`, with no flatness hypothesis.
For $`n\geq 0`, its component of bidegree $`(n,w)` is $`\operatorname{OS}_R^n(\mathcal{A})\otimes_R B_{w-n}`; components of negative first degree vanish.
:::

:::node "lem:small-targets" (decl := OrlikSolomon.affineImage) (decl := OrlikSolomon.zero_notMem_affineImage) (decl := OrlikSolomon.finrank_direction_affineImage_lt) (decl := OrlikSolomon.mem_affineImage_iff_mem_direction) (decl := OrlikSolomon.finrank_direction_affineImage) (decl := OrlikSolomon.normalizedSlice) (decl := OrlikSolomon.finrank_direction_normalizedSlice_add_one) (decl := OrlikSolomon.torusSection) (decl := OrlikSolomon.mem_torusSection) (decl := OrlikSolomon.isLocalization_torusSection) (decl := OrlikSolomon.not_affineImage_le_zeroLocus) (decl := OrlikSolomon.not_normalizedSlice_le_zeroLocus) (decl := OrlikSolomon.toAffineImage) (decl := OrlikSolomon.mapUnits_toAffineImage_torusUnit) (decl := OrlikSolomon.toNormalizedSlice) (decl := OrlikSolomon.mapUnits_toNormalizedSlice_torusUnit) (decl := OrlikSolomon.RelationTarget) (decl := OrlikSolomon.RelationTarget.ofInterEqBot) (decl := OrlikSolomon.RelationTarget.ofDependent)
For equations $`f_1,\ldots,f_s`, the two targets described above exist as arrangement complements, after discarding empty coordinate sections and duplicates, together with the regular maps from $`U_{\mathcal{A}}` to them.
The image and the slice are `affineImage` and `normalizedSlice`, with their dimensions stated in the declarations below, and `torusSection` is the arrangement whose complement is the intersection with $`\mathbb{G}_m^s`.
The maps `toAffineImage` and `toNormalizedSlice` are algebra maps on coordinate rings, sending the coordinate $`y_i` to the unit $`f_i` and to the unit $`f_i/f_1`, so they exist when $`U_{\mathcal{A}}` has no $`k`-points.
The structure `RelationTarget` packages what the two relation lemmas use: a list of units of $`\mathcal{O}(U_{\mathcal{A}})` factoring through the complement of an arrangement on a space of dimension smaller than the length of the list.
The library numbers the functions in the second case from $`0`, so its slice is $`\{y_0=1\}`.
:::

:::node "lem:empty-relation" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.word_logClass_eq_zero) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.log_emptyRelation) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.log_emptyRelation_of_isEquation)
If $`H_1,\ldots,H_s` have empty intersection, then $`[f_1]\cdots[f_s]=0`.
The library states this for the chosen equations, as `log_emptyRelation`, and for any choice of equations, as `log_emptyRelation_of_isEquation`.
Both come from `word_logClass_eq_zero`, which is also the proof of {bp "lem:dependent-relation"}[]: a word of logarithmic classes vanishes as soon as its units factor through the complement of an arrangement on a space of dimension smaller than the length of the word.
:::

:::node "lem:boundary-expansion" (decl := OrlikSolomon.IsExteriorFamily.word_sub_eq_wordBoundary_cons)
For square-zero, pairwise anticommuting elements $`x_1,\ldots,x_s`,
$$`(x_2-x_1)\cdots(x_s-x_1)=\sum_{j=1}^s(-1)^{j-1}x_1\cdots\widehat{x_j}\cdots x_s.`
:::

:::node "lem:dependent-relation" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.word_logClass_eq_zero) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.log_dependentRelation) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.log_dependentRelation_of_isEquation)
For a nonempty intersection of codimension less than $`s`, the boundary relation evaluated on the logarithmic classes is zero.
The hypothesis is `Arrangement.IsDependent`, which contains the nonempty-intersection guard.
:::

:::node "def:realization" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.isFamily_logClass_equationUnit) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.realization) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.realization_generator) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.realization_generator_of_isEquation) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.realization_generator_zeroLocus) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.isHomogeneousOfShift_realization)
The homogeneous algebra map $`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}:\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A})\to C(U_{\mathcal{A}})` sends $`e_H` to $`[f_H]`.
It exists by the universal property of the exterior algebra and the two relation lemmas.
The formula for the generators is `realization_generator` for the chosen equation and `realization_generator_of_isEquation` for any other.
Homogeneity, with $`e_H` in bidegree $`(1,1)` and $`B_w` in bidegree $`(0,w)`, is `isHomogeneousOfShift_realization`.
:::

:::node "lem:realization-basic" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.surjective_realization) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.realization_generator_of_isEquation) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.realization_eq_lift) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.realization_comp_deletionMap) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.realization_comp_map) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.comapEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.SmoothAlg.complementIso) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.realization_comp_comapEquiv)
The map $`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}` is surjective and independent of the chosen equations.
It commutes with the deletion algebra map and the open pullback, with affine coordinate changes, and with relabeling the arrangement.
The label of a generator is its hyperplane, so a relabeling that preserves hyperplanes is an equality of arrangements, which is the case of `realization_comp_map` for an inclusion that is an equality.
The substantive case is a bijection between the hyperplanes of two arrangements given by pullback along an affine equivalence.
The maps `comapEquiv` and `complementIso` take such an equivalence, a coordinate change being the case where the target is the pullback of the source, and `realization_comp_comapEquiv` is the square.
:::
